body:
`scene::build` sets `Canvas::duration` to 0 and makes no keyframes, so a file can only describe a still. The scene model already animates. `Object` carries `opacity: Track<f64>`, a `Track<T>` in `scene/track.rs` holds a constant or keyframes with an `Easing`, and `Scene::draw(t)` lowers the scene at any time `t`. Only Rust code builds keyframes today, as in `crates/acre/tests/scene_examples.rs`.

## Proposal

Jack chose the shape. A `timeline` node in the canvas lists what happens when, by node name:

```text
timeline {
    0s to 1s { scale a 1.5; }
    1s to 2s { move a 0 80; fade b out; }
    2s { show c; }
}
```

- Scale about the node's center, or about a side or corner the node names. Scale and move change the drawing only, so neighbours in a `stack` or `grid` stay put. Strokes and text scale with the node.
- Fade over a span, and `show` and `hide` at an instant. A node shown later starts hidden.
- Change a fill, stroke or text color over a span or at an instant, a row's cell included.
- An arrow or line attached to a node follows the node's moved and scaled edge at every instant, so connectors resolve their ends at draw time.
- The animation lasts until the last change. A canvas `duration` can lengthen it.

Still drawings stay byte-identical. The details of the syntax go on acre-4518a5 as they are pinned down. Scale itself is acre-ec5f6e.
